21:32:59 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Your ears “clog” when the air pressure outside your eardrum changes faster or by more than your Eustachian tubes can equalize the pressure in your middle ears. - **On a plane:** Even though the cabin is pressurized, it is usually maintained at a pressure similar to being about **1,800–2,400 m (6,000–8,000 ft)** above sea level. That is a large pressure change, especially during descent, so your ears may need to “pop.” - **Driving up a mountain:** You may gain thousands of meters of altitude, again creating a substantial drop in outside air pressure. - **In most elevators:** The vertical distance is much smaller—often tens or a few hundred meters—so the pressure change is usually too small to notice. Also, elevators are generally not tightly sealed and may ventilate with air from the building. But it can happen in elevators: in very tall, fast elevators—such as those in major skyscrapers—some people do feel ear popping. The effect depends on both **how much altitude you gain** and **how quickly the pressure changes relative to how easily your Eustachian tubes open**.
