21:33:14 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s fair: for most people, ordinary—even very tall—elevators do **not** produce the same clogged-ear feeling as flying or driving over a mountain pass. The main reason is the **total pressure difference**: - Air pressure falls by only about **1% per 80–85 meters** of height. - A 300 m skyscraper means roughly a **3–4%** pressure change from bottom to top. - Even an 800 m building is only around a **10%** change. - An airplane cabin commonly changes by the equivalent of roughly **2,000–2,400 m** of altitude: about a **20–25%** pressure change. - A mountain drive can likewise involve several thousand meters of elevation change. Your Eustachian tubes normally equalize small changes continuously and unnoticed. In an elevator, even if the ride is fast, the total change is usually small enough that they keep up easily. In a plane—especially during descent—the pressure difference can become large enough that the tubes do not equalize smoothly, creating the blocked sensation, muffled hearing, or pain. A very tall elevator might make some people notice a tiny “pop,” particularly if they have a cold, allergies, or naturally narrow Eustachian tubes. But **actual ear clogging from elevators is not a common experience**, so your experience is entirely typical.
